  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I reconciled all five patients against their St. Brigid discharge documents under RBC-TOC-02 and wrote a progress note for each. Results, a corrected discharge summary and an unsigned draft arrived while I worked; three of them changed decisions I had already made, so I fixed those and wrote correction notes.\n\n**Walter Kim**\n- **Furosemide:** changed from 20 mg daily to 40 mg twice daily. The signed addendum corrected the discharge summary's \"40 mg daily.\"\n- **Potassium chloride:** stopped, as the discharge summary says.\n- **Omeprazole:** kept. The pantoprazole on the discharge list was a hospital formulary substitution, so I didn't order it.\n- **Metoprolol XL, atorvastatin, aspirin:** continued with no change.\n- **Lisinopril:** at first I kept it on hold because potassium was 5.4 on 10/21. A repeat panel came in today showing potassium 4.7 and creatinine 1.21, which meets the resume condition (creatinine under 1.5), so I restarted it.\n- **Task to disregard:** the task I'd made for you to review his potassium (task-t0003) is no longer needed. I couldn't cancel it from here, so please close it.\n- **Draft addendum:** an unsigned draft proposing furosemide 60 mg twice daily also arrived. Only signed addenda count, so I didn't act on it.\n\n**Terence Stark**\n- **Carvedilol (Coreg):** I first raised it to 12.5 mg twice daily per the discharge summary. A signed addendum then arrived saying the increase was reversed before discharge (heart rate 48–52, lightheaded on standing). I changed it back to 6.25 mg twice daily.\n- **Aspirin, insulin 70/30:** continued with no change.\n- **Flagged to you by message:**\n  - **Atorvastatin 80 mg:** his fills cover only about 24% of the 180 days before admission (the last supply ran out around 6/1), below the 80% threshold. The discharge summary says continue, but the policy says not to change it, so I left the order as is.\n  - **Glipizide 5 mg:** an outside prescriber (Dr. Desai) has been filling it monthly since July, but the discharge summary doesn't mention it. I didn't order or change anything. Please check whether he should take it alongside insulin 70/30.\n\n**Earnest Roberts**\n- **Lisinopril:** I restarted it because creatinine was 1.08 (under 1.3) and potassium 4.6 on 10/22. A new potassium of 5.5 arrived today, which is above the 5.0 limit, so I put it back on hold. I made an urgent task for you due 10/26 to review the repeat potassium. He may need to be told to keep holding lisinopril in the meantime.\n- **Insulin glargine, insulin lispro, rosuvastatin:** continued with no change.\n\n**Gerald Whitcomb**\n- **Lisinopril 20 mg:** restarted. Creatinine was 1.12 (under 1.5) and potassium 4.4 on 10/21.\n- **Amlodipine, atorvastatin:** continued with no change.\n- **Pending lab:** the potassium the discharge plan asked for 3 days after discharge isn't in the chart yet.\n\n**Lorraine Pruitt**\n- **Metformin:** stays on hold. Its only creatinine (1.21) was drawn in the hospital, so it doesn't meet the \"post-discharge labs\" condition. I made a task for you due 10/24, when the post-discharge panel is expected.\n- **Amlodipine, atorvastatin:** continued with no change.\n\nI closed the four new inbox items for these patients. No other new items came in for them.",
